---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (url) => `${base}${url.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Corrections Policy | Plain Act"
  description="How Plain Act receives, reviews, corrects, and documents material errors in published management content."
  path="/corrections-policy/"
>
  <section class="hero policy-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[{name:'Home', url:'/'}, {name:'Corrections Policy', url:'/corrections-policy/'}]} />
      <p class="eyebrow">Corrections policy</p>
      <h1>Errors should be easy to report and clear to fix.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Plain Act reviews reported factual errors, broken references, outdated details, misleading wording, and material omissions. This policy explains what can be corrected, how reports are evaluated, and how dates are handled.</p>
      <p class="small">Policy reviewed: August 31, 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-sm">
    <div class="container corrections-grid">
      <article class="card flat">
        <span class="policy-number">01</span>
        <h2>How to report a correction</h2>
        <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> and include:</p>
        <ul class="list-clean"><li>the page URL</li><li>the exact statement, table, link, or section</li><li>what you believe is incorrect or misleading</li><li>a supporting source or evidence when available</li></ul>
        <p>A concise report makes the issue easier to reproduce and review.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card flat">
        <span class="policy-number">02</span>
        <h2>What qualifies for review</h2>
        <p>Examples include incorrect facts, outdated platform information, broken or misrepresented references, wrong product or publication metadata, a misleading factual implication, or an omission that materially changes how a factual statement should be understood.</p>
        <p>Disagreement with an editorial judgment does not automatically make it a factual error. However, unclear wording may still be revised when it creates avoidable confusion.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card flat">
        <span class="policy-number">03</span>
        <h2>How a report is evaluated</h2>
        <p>The reported text is checked against the source, current product behavior, or other relevant evidence when that evidence is available. The review may also check whether the same issue appears in related pages, metadata, internal links, or structured data.</p>
        <p>A report may be closed without a change when the published statement remains supported or when the requested change is an opinion rather than a correction.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card flat">
        <span class="policy-number">04</span>
        <h2>How material corrections are handled</h2>
        <p>When an issue is confirmed, the affected page may be corrected, clarified, expanded, noindexed, redirected, or removed depending on the problem. Related links, metadata, or other pages may also be changed if the error affects them.</p>
        <p>Plain Act does not preserve an error merely to avoid changing an already published page.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card flat">
        <span class="policy-number">05</span>
        <h2>Dates and change notes</h2>
        <p>A page should not display a fabricated publication, review, or update date. If a material review or correction occurs and the page carries an updated date, that date should reflect the real event.</p>
        <p>Not every spelling, formatting, or layout correction requires a public correction note. Material factual changes may be described on the page when doing so helps the reader understand what changed.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card flat">
        <span class="policy-number">06</span>
        <h2>Response timing</h2>
        <p>Plain Act does not promise immediate correction, a fixed response time, or continuous monitoring of every page. Reports are reviewed as publishing capacity allows, with priority given to issues that materially affect factual accuracy, user understanding, or a current external rule or product detail.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-sm">
    <div class="container notice correction-contact">
      <h2>Send a correction report</h2>
      <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> with the page URL and the exact issue. For the wider publication standards, read the <a href={href('/editorial-policy/')}>Editorial Policy</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .policy-hero h1{max-width:15ch}.corrections-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}.corrections-grid article{position:relative;padding-top:3.5rem}.policy-number{position:absolute;right:1.2rem;top:1.15rem;color:var(--accent);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.12em}.corrections-grid h2{max-width:15ch}.correction-contact{max-width:900px}
  @media(max-width:800px){.corrections-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
